    <body>
      <sec id="article-27120.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Phenylketonuria, also known as phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency, is an inborn error of metabolism most commonly caused by pathogenic or pathogenic variants in the gene encoding phenylalanine hydroxylase&#x02014;the hepatic enzyme that converts phenylalanine to tyrosine. Phenylketonuria is classified as an aminoacidopathy, or toxic accumulation disorder, in which excess substrate, specifically phenylalanine and its metabolites, produces neurotoxicity if untreated. Biochemically, phenylketonuria is characterized by elevated blood phenylalanine&#x000a0;concentrations and a relative tyrosine&#x000a0;deficiency. Affected newborns typically appear normal at birth; however, without treatment, progressive manifestations develop over the first months of life and may include a musty or mousy odor of the skin or urine, hypopigmentation, eczema, irritability, tremors, seizures, and neurocognitive impairment.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the genetic and biochemical basis of phenylketonuria and recognize its clinical presentation across the lifespan.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply&#x000a0;current American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics guideline recommendations for diagnosis, monitoring, and treatment of patients with phenylketonuria.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate traditional and emerging therapies, including dietary management, pharmacologic options, and investigational gene and messenger RNA&#x02013;based approaches.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement effective interprofessional strategies to coordinate care, communicate management plans, and support long-term adherence for individuals with phenylketonuria.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27120&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27120">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Phenylketonuria (PKU), also known as phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) deficiency, is one of the earliest recognized inborn errors of metabolism and remains a paradigm for diagnosing and treating genetic metabolic diseases. First described by the Norwegian biochemist and physician Asbj&#x000f8;rn F&#x000f8;lling in the 1930s, based on observations of abnormal phenylalanine metabolites in affected children, PKU later became the foundation for modern newborn screening programs, with the development of a simple bacterial inhibition assay in the 1960s.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r1">[1]</xref> PKU&#x000a0;is the most common disorder caused by an inborn error of amino acid metabolism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Early detection and dietary intervention have transformed PKU from a cause of severe intellectual disability into a largely treatable condition, underscoring the critical importance of timely recognition and lifelong metabolic management.</p>
        <p>PKU is most commonly caused by mutations in the gene encoding PAH, a primarily hepatic enzyme that catalyzes the hydroxylation of phenylalanine to tyrosine (see <bold>Image.</bold> PKU and Role of PAH, BH4, Melanin, and Phenyl Ketones).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;The disorder is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, and pathogenic variants reduce or eliminate PAH activity in affected individuals. Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) serves as an essential cofactor by binding to the catalytic domain of PAH and enabling this metabolic conversion. PKU is classified as an aminoacidopathy, or toxic accumulation disorder, in which excess substrate&#x02014;phenylalanine and its metabolites&#x02014;produce neurotoxicity if untreated. Biochemically, PKU is characterized by elevated blood phenylalanine concentrations and a relative tyrosine deficiency.</p>
        <p>Affected newborns typically appear normal at birth; however, without treatment, progressive manifestations emerge over the first months of life and may include a musty odor of the skin or urine, irritability, tremors, seizures, microcephaly, and neurocognitive impairment. These neurological and developmental features reflect the developing brain's vulnerability to sustained hyperphenylalaninemia (HPA). Tyrosine is a precursor of the skin pigment melanin, and reduced tyrosine causes hypopigmentation of the skin and hair. The musty odor in individuals with PKU is caused by phenylacetic acid, a metabolite of phenylalanine that accumulates and is excreted in sweat, urine, and skin secretions.</p>
        <p>This activity reviews the etiology, epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, evaluation, and management of PKU, emphasizing early detection through newborn screening and lifelong metabolic control. This activity incorporates the 2025 American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics guideline&#x02013;based recommendations, highlights recent therapeutic advances, and discusses emerging investigational approaches, such as gene and messenger RNA therapies, aimed at restoring PAH activity. This activity&#x000a0;emphasizes the role of coordinated, interprofessional care in optimizing outcomes across the lifespan.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>PKU&#x000a0;is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the <italic toggle="yes">PAH</italic> gene, which encodes the enzyme PAH. This autosomal recessive disorder results in deficient PAH activity and impaired conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine, leading to the biochemical phenotype of elevated blood phenylalanine levels and relative tyrosine deficiency. Carriers with a single functional allele typically maintain sufficient enzymatic activity to prevent disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">PAH</italic>&#x000a0;gene is located on chromosome 12q23.2, and hundreds of likely pathogenic&#x000a0;or pathogenic variants have been identified worldwide, most of which reduce PAH activity through protein misfolding, instability, or decreased expression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;As a result, phenylalanine cannot be adequately metabolized, leading to its accumulation and reduced tyrosine production. Under normal conditions, phenylalanine, an essential amino acid, is tightly regulated to support protein synthesis while remaining at nontoxic concentrations.</p>
        <p>The most common pathogenic variant replaces arginine with tryptophan at codon 408 (p.Arg408Trp) and is strongly associated with classic PKU, a traditional PAH terminology, in which blood phenylalanine levels&#x000a0;typically exceed 1200 &#x000b5;mol/L.&#x000a0;Some&#x000a0;variants retain residual enzyme activity and are associated with less severe phenotypes, including mild PKU (blood phenylalanine levels of 600 to 1200 &#x000b5;mol/L) and mild&#x000a0;HPA (blood phenylalanine levels below 600 &#x000b5;mol/L).&#x000a0;Approximately 73% of individuals with PKU are compound heterozygotes, carrying&#x000a0;2 distinct&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">PAH</italic>&#x000a0;variants rather than identical&#x000a0;or homozygous variants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;About 27% are homozygous for a single pathogenic&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">PAH</italic>&#x000a0;variant.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Genotype combinations influence metabolic severity, such as severe-severe or severe-mild allele pairings, contributing to the clinical spectrum of disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Overall, PKU is a disorder of impaired enzymatic function with significant allelic heterogeneity, and the specific genetic variants largely determine residual PAH activity, biochemical phenotype, and therapeutic responsiveness.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>PKU has a global prevalence estimated at approximately 1 in 10,000 to 24,000 live births,&#x000a0;with rates varying widely by ethnicity and geographic region, ranging from 1 in 4500 in Italy to 1 in 125,000 in Japan. An estimated 450,000 individuals worldwide are affected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Europe: Overall prevalence is roughly 1 in 10,000 live births, with notable regional variation. Turkey reports the highest rates (1 in 2600&#x000a0;to 1 in 4000), which are believed to be at least partly attributable to higher consanguinity. Northern Ireland and Italy each report a prevalence of 1 in 4500, whereas Finland has among the lowest prevalence (1 in 100,000&#x000a0;to 1 in 200,000). A geographic gradient exists, with classic PKU more common in Eastern Europe and milder phenotypes (mild PKU and HPA) predominating in southwestern and southern Europe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>United States: Incidence is approximately 1 in 15,000 live births, with state-specific rates ranging from 1 in 13,500 to 1 in 19,000.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;PKU is more frequent among Whites and Native Americans and less common among Black, Hispanic, and Asian populations. Over 10,000 individuals are estimated to have PKU in the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r11">[11]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Asia: Prevalence varies widely from 1 in 15,000 to 1 in 100,500. China reports 1 in 15,000&#x000a0;to 100,500; Japan, 1 in 70,000&#x000a0;to 125,000; and Thailand, less than 1 in 200,000.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Latin America: Reported prevalence ranges from 1 in 25,000 to 1 in 50,000, with higher rates in southern regions.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Africa: PKU appears rare, though precise data are limited.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Phenotype Distribution</bold>
</p>
        <p>Globally, approximately 62% of cases are classic PKU, 22% are mild PKU, and 16% are mild HPA. The proportion of classic PKU increases from Europe (56%) to the Middle East (71%) to Australia (80%), reflecting population-specific patterns of <italic toggle="yes">PAH</italic> variants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Carrier Frequency</bold>
</p>
        <p>Carrier frequency depends on ethnicity and is estimated at approximately 1 in 50 among individuals of Northern European descent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>PKU results from a deficiency of PAH, the rate-limiting hepatic enzyme that converts phenylalanine to tyrosine and requires BH4, molecular oxygen, and iron as cofactors. Loss of PAH activity due to biallelic pathogenic variants impairs phenylalanine metabolism, leading to the accumulation of phenylalanine and its toxic metabolites&#x02014;phenylpyruvate, phenylacetate, and phenylactate&#x02014;in the blood and brain, along with a relative tyrosine deficiency.&#x000a0;The disorder is characterized by both substrate toxicity and a deficiency of downstream products, including catecholamine neurotransmitters, melanin, and other tyrosine-derived compounds.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Elevated phenylalanine readily crosses the blood-brain barrier and disrupts cerebral amino acid transport by competitively inhibiting the uptake of other large neutral amino acids, such as tyrosine and tryptophan. This inhibition reduces brain concentrations of these precursors and impairs the synthesis of key neurotransmitters, including dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine, and serotonin, contributing to abnormalities in neurodevelopment and function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Tyrosine deficiency also reduces melanin production, explaining the hypopigmentation often observed in untreated individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>The neurologic manifestations of PKU arise from multiple interconnected mechanisms and are not solely due to phenylalanine toxicity. Proposed contributors include altered neurotransmitter metabolism, decreased cerebral protein synthesis, impaired bioenergetics, oxidative stress, and white matter abnormalities. The severity of clinical disease correlates with the degree of residual PAH activity, determined by the specific genotype, producing a spectrum from classic PKU to mild HPA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Some less severe variants encode enzymes with altered affinity for the PAH cofactor BH4 or demonstrate increased stability in the presence of BH4. This molecular variability helps explain why an estimated 49% of patients respond to pharmacologic BH4 supplementation, typically defined as a reduction of at least 30% in blood phenylalanine levels despite normal endogenous BH4 concentrations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Overall, the pathophysiology of PKU reflects a loss-of-function metabolic block that disrupts phenylalanine homeostasis, leading to toxic metabolite accumulation and a deficiency of critical neurochemical substrates during vulnerable periods of brain development.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Infants with PKU appear normal at birth, and diagnosis is typically made through routine screening before symptoms develop. Universal newborn screening has largely prevented the classic untreated phenotype in&#x000a0;many countries. Without early identification and treatment, clinical signs and symptoms emerge gradually over the first months to years of life.</p>
        <p>
<bold>History in Untreated or Late-Diagnosed Phenylketonuria Cases</bold>
</p>
        <p>Historical features reflect progressive neurotoxicity from elevated phenylalanine levels. Developmental delay typically&#x000a0;appears between 6 and 12 months of age, followed by progressive intellectual disability that can become severe and irreversible if untreated. Caregivers may report seizures, including infantile spasms; behavioral concerns such as hyperactivity, aggression, self-injury, or autistic features; and later psychiatric symptoms such as anxiety or depression. Motor abnormalities such as tremor, spasticity, and gait disturbances may also develop.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>History in Early-Treated Phenylketonuria Cases</bold>
</p>
        <p>Individuals identified by newborn screening and treated early typically do not develop neurologic complications. However, even with good metabolic control, some patients report subtle executive function difficulties; attention problems; slower processing speed; mood symptoms such as anxiety or depression; and challenges maintaining long-term dietary adherence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Family History</bold>
</p>
        <p>Because PKU is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, clinicians should obtain a detailed family history, including affected siblings, known carrier status, and parental consanguinity, all of which increase the likelihood of the condition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination Findings in Untreated Phenylketonuria</bold>
</p>
        <p>The classic phenotype, now rarely observed because of newborn screening, includes hypopigmentation, resulting in fair skin, light hair, and blue eyes due to reduced melanin synthesis from tyrosine deficiency.&#x000a0;Head circumference is normal at birth but may fall below expected percentiles over time, resulting in acquired microcephaly. A characteristic musty body odor arises from phenylacetic acid excreted in sweat and urine.&#x000a0;Additional findings may include eczematous skin changes, growth impairment, and neurologic abnormalities such as&#x000a0;diminished reflexes, hypotonia, spasticity, and tremor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination Findings in Early-Treated Phenylketonuria</bold>
</p>
        <p>In patients diagnosed and treated shortly after birth, the physical examination is&#x000a0;typically normal. Some individuals may have slightly lighter pigmentation than unaffected family members, and subtle neurologic findings, such as mild tremor or hyperreflexia, have been reported in adolescents and adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>In the United States and many other countries, PKU is first identified through universal newborn screening programs. Screening is performed on dried blood spots obtained by heel prick at approximately 24 to 48 hours of life and analyzed by tandem mass spectrometry to measure phenylalanine concentration and the phenylalanine-to-tyrosine molar ratio.</p>
        <p>Neonates with a positive screen require prompt diagnostic confirmation. Quantitative plasma amino acid analysis should be obtained to verify&#x000a0;elevated phenylalanine levels, calculate the phenylalanine-to-tyrosine ratio, and assess the overall amino acid profile. These measurements help distinguish true&#x000a0;HPA from transient or false-positive screening results.</p>
        <p>Because disorders of BH4 metabolism can also present with elevated phenylalanine levels but require fundamentally different management, evaluation must include biochemical testing to exclude BH4 deficiency.&#x000a0;This evaluation typically involves analysis of blood or urine pterins and, when indicated, assessment of dihydropteridine reductase activity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Identifying a BH4-related disorder is critical because treatment requires neurotransmitter replacement and BH4 supplementation rather than dietary therapy alone.</p>
        <p>Current recommendations from the American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics emphasize including PAH genotyping in the diagnostic workup. Genotype information can help predict disease severity and the likelihood of responsiveness to specific therapies, thereby improving individualized treatment planning. Many affected individuals are compound heterozygotes, and identifying pathogenic variants may guide the selection of pharmacologic options and eligibility for emerging therapies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>In addition to its diagnostic value, molecular testing can support family counseling. Targeted genotyping or carrier screening panels may identify pathogenic variants in relatives or prospective parents, thereby clarifying reproductive risk in this autosomal recessive condition, in which 2 carrier parents have a 25% chance of having an affected child.</p>
        <p>Overall, evaluation following an abnormal screen aims to confirm HPA, distinguish PAH deficiency from BH4-related disorders, and obtain molecular information&#x000a0;that alters prognosis, therapeutic selection, and genetic counseling.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Treatment of PKU focuses on lowering blood phenylalanine concentrations to prevent neurocognitive impairment. Recommended lifelong target phenylalanine levels are 120&#x000a0;to 360 &#x003bc;mol/L.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r15">[15]</xref> Management is lifelong and requires coordinated metabolic, nutritional, and psychosocial care.&#x000a0;Ensuring equitable access to pharmacologic therapies and ongoing specialized dietetic support remains critical to achieving optimal long-term outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Dietary Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>A phenylalanine-restricted diet remains the cornerstone of PKU therapy and should be initiated within 10 to 14 days of birth and continued lifelong.&#x000a0;Early diagnosis and treatment are highly effective in preventing the severe intellectual disability historically associated with untreated classic PKU.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Dietary therapy includes:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoidance of high-protein foods such as meat, fish, eggs, dairy and soy products, and nuts and products containing aspartame, which contain high amounts of phenylalanine</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Careful measurement of the limited natural protein intake from low-protein foods such as selected fruits and vegetables&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use of phenylalanine-free or low-phenylalanine medical formulas to provide adequate protein, tyrosine, vitamins, and minerals&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Incorporation of specialized low-protein foods to improve caloric intake and dietary variety</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Dietary therapy for infants with PKU must begin immediately after newborn screening confirms HPA, ideally within the first 7 to 10 days of life. Early initiation is critical because treatment delays are associated with measurable reductions in later cognitive outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>Initial management often includes a brief washout period with an exclusive phenylalanine-free formula until blood phenylalanine levels normalize, followed by gradual reintroduction of measured amounts of natural protein to determine individual tolerance. In classic PKU, normalization typically occurs within about 1 week, whereas patients with milder forms may stabilize more rapidly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Treatment is recommended when blood phenylalanine levels exceed 360 &#x003bc;mol/L, with a target range of 120 to 360 &#x003bc;mol/L during childhood.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>The infant diet consists of 3 primary components:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Phenylalanine-free amino acid formula, which provides the majority of protein and energy requirements&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Measured amounts of natural protein, supplying essential phenylalanine in quantities individualized to tolerance (approximately 200 mg/d in classic PKU during early childhood)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Low-protein specialty foods, introduced as solids are added to the diet&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Breastfeeding is encouraged and safe when combined with a phenylalanine-free formula. Because breast milk contains relatively low phenylalanine concentrations, it can serve as a controlled source of natural protein. Studies show that breastfed infants with PKU maintain appropriate metabolic control and demonstrate favorable growth outcomes. Common approaches include alternating breastfeeding with phenylalanine-free formula or providing measured amounts of expressed breast milk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>The Special Supplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants, and Children (WIC) in the United States provides infant formulas and medical foods for qualifying conditions, including inborn errors of metabolism such as PKU. However, access to medical foods remains inconsistent. A review in <italic toggle="yes">Pediatrics</italic> noted that coverage for medical foods is not uniformly ensured despite their established efficacy, and many insurance plans provide inadequate reimbursement. Surveys of affected families report ongoing financial burden, with some incurring high out-of-pocket costs for medical foods and related treatment. Limited coverage and financial barriers are recognized contributors to dietary nonadherence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>When appropriately supplemented with phenylalanine-free amino acid formulas, the low-phenylalanine diet can meet protein requirements for growth and maintenance across the lifespan. Total protein intake should meet age-based requirements, with an additional proportion provided as phenylalanine-free L-amino acid supplements to compensate for reduced utilization of elemental amino acids and to support metabolic control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Infants and children have higher protein requirements per kilogram to support growth. In adults managed with traditional dietary therapy, a substantial proportion of total protein intake comes from amino acid supplements. Adults treated with pegvaliase who liberalize their diets may meet protein requirements through dietary protein sources while still maintaining normal protein status.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>Adequate amino acid supplementation is essential. Insufficient intake can lead to net protein catabolism, resulting in the release of endogenous phenylalanine from tissue breakdown and paradoxically increasing blood phenylalanine levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Although effective, strict dietary therapy is challenging, particularly during adolescence and adulthood, when nonadherence often increases due to social and lifestyle factors. In addition, long-term dietary restriction has been associated with micronutrient deficiencies, including selenium, copper, magnesium, and zinc,&#x000a0;which should be monitored and supplemented as indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pharmacologic Therapies</bold>
</p>
        <p>In addition to dietary restriction, pharmacologic options are available for selected patients. Three medications are currently approved for PKU treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Sapropterin dihydrochloride, a synthetic form of BH4, enhances residual PAH activity in responsive individuals. This drug was&#x000a0;Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-approved for use in patients aged 1 month and older in December 2007, making it the first pharmacologic agent approved for the treatment of PKU in the United States. Approximately 20% to 55% of patients&#x02014;primarily those with milder forms of PKU&#x02014;respond to treatment. Treatment can reduce blood phenylalanine levels by approximately 29% and increase dietary protein tolerance. Sapropterin can enable more flexible and sustainable dietary management in some children with PKU, facilitating improved metabolic control, normal growth, and enhanced quality of life, including the ability to eat&#x000a0;a more varied diet with&#x000a0;their families.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Response varies by genotype, and patients with classic PKU are less likely to benefit. Because responsiveness cannot always be predicted, a therapeutic trial (often 30 days) may be undertaken to assess benefit. Sapropterin therapy has also been associated with improvements in brain function in some patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Sepiapterin is a natural precursor of BH4 with greater oral bioavailability than sapropterin. Through a dual mechanism of action, sepiapterin increases intracellular BH4 levels and stabilizes the PAH enzyme, thereby enhancing residual PAH activity and lowering blood phenylalanine concentrations. Sepiapterin received FDA approval in July 2025 for the treatment of HPA in adult and pediatric patients 1 month of age and older with sepiapterin-responsive PKU, making it the third FDA-approved pharmacologic therapy for this condition<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Approval was supported by the phase 3 APHENITY trial, in which 84% of sepiapterin-treated patients achieved blood phenylalanine levels below 360 &#x000b5;mol/L, compared with 9% of placebo recipients. Over 70% of enrolled participants had a genotype-phenotype consistent with classic PKU. Notably, 43% of individuals previously unresponsive to sapropterin demonstrated a response to sepiapterin, expanding the eligible treatment population beyond that of existing BH4-based therapy. In the open-label extension, over 97% of participants demonstrated the ability to liberalize their diet while maintaining metabolic control, with a mean increase in protein intake of 126%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Sepiapterin is administered orally as a powder formulation and is indicated for use in conjunction with a phenylalanine-restricted diet. As with sapropterin, biochemical responsiveness can only be determined through a therapeutic trial with careful monitoring of dietary and nutritional balance. Common adverse reactions include diarrhea, headache, abdominal pain, hypophenylalaninemia, and fecal discoloration. Concomitant use of dihydrofolate reductase inhibitors, such as trimethoprim and methotrexate, should be avoided, as these agents may reduce sepiapterin metabolism to BH4.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Pegvaliase is a PEGylated phenylalanine ammonia lyase, which is administered through a daily subcutaneous injection. Phenylalanine ammonia lyase is a nonhuman enzyme that converts phenylalanine into trans-cinnamic acid and ammonia, thereby providing an alternative metabolic pathway for phenylalanine disposal. Through PEGylation (the attachment of polyethylene glycol), the enzyme's stability and circulating half-life are increased. Pegvaliase acts as an enzyme-substitution therapy by degrading phenylalanine independently of PAH activity. Pegvaliase is approved in the United States for adolescents and adults (aged 12 or older) and in Europe for adolescent and adult patients (aged&#x000a0;16 or older) with blood phenylalanine levels &#x0003e;600 &#x003bc;mol/L who are inadequately controlled on existing management. Historically, direct enzyme replacement targeting PAH was not feasible because of enzyme instability; pegvaliase represents a successful enzyme-substitution strategy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Unlike sapropterin, pegvaliase can be effective across disease severities. However, treatment requires prolonged dose titration and close monitoring because of the risk of hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;The long-term impact of pegvaliase on dietary liberalization and cognitive outcomes remains under evaluation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Adjunct and Emerging Therapies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Large neutral amino acids may be used as adjunctive therapy in adolescents and adults. These agents competitively inhibit phenylalanine transport across the blood-brain barrier, potentially lowering cerebral phenylalanine concentrations.</p>
        <p>Gene therapy has advanced and is now in active clinical development. As of 2026, multiple phase 1/2 trials are evaluating gene-based approaches for PKU. Adeno-associated virus vector&#x02013;based therapies aim to deliver functional PAH genes to hepatocytes to restore enzymatic activity. Early studies have demonstrated proof of concept, with some participants achieving sustained reductions in blood phenylalanine levels.&#x000a0;However, challenges remain, including durability of response, immune reactions to Adeno-associated virus vectors, and the potential need for immunosuppression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>Additionally, messenger RNA&#x02013;based therapies that use lipid nanoparticles to deliver PAH messenger RNA to hepatocytes are under investigation. This strategy enables the transient expression of functional PAH protein and may&#x000a0;allow repeat dosing while avoiding the genomic integration concerns associated with viral vector&#x02013;based approaches, such as insertional mutagenesis, unintended disruption of host genes, and the irreversibility of permanent DNA modification.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>Although promising, gene therapy for PKU remains investigational as of 2026, with ongoing efforts focused on optimizing vector design,&#x000a0;duration of effect, immune management, and long-term safety.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Maternal Phenylketonuria</bold>
</p>
        <p>Women of childbearing age with PKU require preconception counseling and strict metabolic control before and during pregnancy. Elevated maternal phenylalanine levels are teratogenic and can cause fetal brain injury and congenital heart disease, regardless of whether the fetus has PKU.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists recommends normalizing maternal phenylalanine levels to &#x0003c;360 &#x003bc;mol/L for at least 3 months before conception and maintaining levels of 120&#x000a0;to 360 &#x003bc;mol/L throughout pregnancy to reduce the risk of maternal PKU embryopathy. Early control&#x02014;ideally before conception or by 8 weeks of gestation&#x02014;is critical, as fetal cardiac development occurs by 8 to 10 weeks. Without treatment, offspring are at high risk for developmental delay, microcephaly, congenital heart defects, growth restriction, and seizures. However, excessively low maternal phenylalanine levels, particularly later in pregnancy, may contribute to intrauterine growth restriction and warrant close monitoring. Management should include consultation with a metabolic geneticist and a maternal-fetal medicine specialist, with tight metabolic surveillance throughout pregnancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Overall, PKU management requires lifelong monitoring, individualized dietary and pharmacologic strategies, and attention to developmental, nutritional, and reproductive health.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>HPA, identified by elevated phenylalanine levels on newborn screening, can result from several conditions that require distinct management strategies. Prompt differentiation is critical, particularly to identify disorders that do not respond to dietary therapy alone.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Phenylalanine Hydroxylase Deficiency Spectrum</bold>
</p>
        <p>PAH deficiency is the most common cause of HPA, accounting for approximately 98% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Classic PKU: Untreated phenylalanine levels &#x0003e;1200 &#x003bc;mol/L; requires strict lifelong dietary management.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mild PKU: Untreated phenylalanine levels of 600&#x000a0;to 1200 &#x003bc;mol/L; dietary treatment is usually required.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mild HPA: Phenylalanine levels of&#x000a0;120&#x000a0;to 600 &#x003bc;mol/L; may not require treatment.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Tetrahydrobiopterin Deficiencies</bold>
</p>
        <p>BH4 deficiencies are rare, accounting for only 1% to 2% of HPA cases.&#x000a0;These deficiencies are critical to identify because they cause progressive neurological deterioration&#x000a0;in patients&#x000a0;treated with dietary therapy alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r37">[37]</xref> BH4 deficiencies require supplementation with BH4 and neurotransmitter precursors. Biochemical testing includes measuring blood or urine pterins and, in some cases, dihydropteridine reductase&#x000a0;activity. Patients with BH4 deficiency also have elevated prolactin levels, which occur because dopamine normally inhibits prolactin secretion, and BH4 deficiency impairs dopamine synthesis, resulting in hyperprolactinemia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Transient&#x000a0;Hyperphenylalaninemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Transiently elevated phenylalanine levels occur primarily in premature infants due to delayed maturation of the PAH enzyme system. Levels normalize spontaneously, and neurological outcomes are typically unaffected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Secondary Causes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Other conditions can result in elevated phenylalanine,&#x000a0;usually in the context of broader metabolic derangements:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Liver disease: Generalized amino acid elevation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Total parenteral nutrition: Elevated amino acids due to exogenous intake.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Maternal PKU embryopathy: The infant may have normal phenylalanine but presents with microcephaly, congenital heart defects, or developmental delay from in utero exposure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis for PKU is excellent with early diagnosis and lifelong treatment, although outcomes may remain modestly below those of the general population. When therapy is initiated within the first 10 to 14 days of life and metabolic control is maintained, most individuals achieve intelligence within normal limits, complete standard educational milestones, maintain employment, and lead independent adult lives. Life expectancy is normal with appropriate treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Untreated Phenylketonuria</bold>
</p>
        <p>Without treatment, PKU results in severe, irreversible intellectual disability, microcephaly, epilepsy, behavioral disturbances, psychiatric symptoms, motor abnormalities, eczematous rash, and autism spectrum features. The neurologic injury caused by sustained&#x000a0;HPA is permanent and cannot be reversed once established.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Early-Treated Phenylketonuria</bold>
</p>
        <p>Newborn screening and early dietary intervention have been highly effective in preventing the profound neurologic sequelae of classic PKU. However, even with early and continuous treatment, subtle neurocognitive differences may persist. Meta-analyses show a 1.9- to 4.1-point reduction in IQ for every 100 &#x003bc;mol/L increase in mean lifetime phenylalanine concentration among early-treated individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>Commonly affected domains include executive function, processing speed, attention, verbal memory, and learning. A recent review also highlights persistent executive function deficits despite early treatment. These impairments are generally mild and do not preclude normal educational attainment or employment. Neuroimaging often shows white matter abnormalities and altered myelination, although the clinical significance of these findings remains uncertain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Quality of Life and Psychosocial Outcomes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Overall quality of life in early-treated adults is good and comparable to that of control populations, with no significant increase in major psychiatric disorders or social dysfunction. Educational attainment, occupational outcomes, emotional functioning, and family relationships are generally similar to those of unaffected peers, with only minor differences in relationship style reported. Standard quality-of-life instruments may underestimate the burdens of PKU, prompting the development of disease-specific assessment tools.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Factors Affecting Prognosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Long-term outcomes are strongly associated with early initiation of therapy and consistent metabolic control throughout life. Dietary adherence often becomes more challenging during adolescence and adulthood, potentially influencing cognitive and psychosocial outcomes. Notably, individual variability exists&#x02014;some patients experience neurocognitive effects despite good metabolic control. In contrast, others remain relatively unaffected despite suboptimal control, suggesting additional susceptibility factors that are not yet fully understood.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r44">[44]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of PKU differ markedly between untreated individuals and those diagnosed and treated in the first weeks of life. Although untreated disease leads to severe, irreversible neurologic injury, treated patients may experience metabolic, neurologic, psychiatric, skeletal, and nutritional complications&#x02014;particularly when metabolic control is suboptimal.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Untreated Phenylketonuria</bold>
</p>
        <p>Untreated PKU causes progressive neurologic injury due to the toxic accumulation of phenylalanine in the brain, resulting in impaired neurotransmitter synthesis, disrupted myelination, and white matter damage. Clinical manifestations include epilepsy, motor disturbances, behavioral abnormalities, and psychiatric symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The timing of phenylalanine exposure influences manifestations, with fetal exposure affecting brain and cardiac development and later exposure contributing to attention deficits, speech delay, and behavioral disturbances.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Treated Phenylketonuria</bold>
</p>
        <p>Even with early treatment, complications may occur, particularly when phenylalanine levels are elevated or dietary adherence is poor.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Neurologic Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>In adults&#x02014;especially those who discontinue dietary therapy&#x02014;neurologic findings may include tremor (most common), hyperreflexia, cerebellar ataxia, balance impairment, migraines, sensory symptoms, and visual disturbances. A French cohort study reported neurologic symptoms in 11.2% of early-treated adults, confined to those with classic PKU. These manifestations are frequently associated with white matter lesions (leukoencephalopathy) on magnetic resonance imaging and correlate with elevated phenylalanine levels. Importantly, many neurologic symptoms improve or resolve with restoration of metabolic control.&#x000a0;Autonomic dysfunction and neurophysiologic abnormalities, including prolonged motor-evoked potential latencies, have also been described in treated adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Psychiatric and Behavioral Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Depression and anxiety disorders affect approximately 25.7% of treated adults, although the relationship to phenylalanine levels remains incompletely defined. Mood disturbances and behavioral abnormalities are also reported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Skeletal Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Reduced bone mineral density is more common in individuals with PKU. A meta-analysis reports mean Z-scores of &#x02212;0.45 for total bone mineral density, &#x02212;0.70 for the lumbar spine, and &#x02212;0.96 for the femoral neck. The skeletal defect appears mixed, with reduced cortical thickness, impaired trabecular bone structure, and decreased bone strength relative to muscle force. Osteopenia and osteoporosis are more prevalent than in the general population, though fracture risk has been inconsistently demonstrated. Contributing mechanisms include HPA-related effects on bone metabolism, nutritional deficiencies (particularly calcium and vitamin D), and dietary nonadherence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Nutritional Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nutritional deficiencies may occur, particularly when intake of medical formulas is inconsistent. Vitamin B12 deficiency is a recognized risk in patients following a low-protein diet without adequate supplementation. Other reported micronutrient deficiencies include selenium, zinc, coenzyme Q10, and L-carnitine, which may contribute to oxidative stress in patients with poorly controlled disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Elevated cerebrospinal fluid levels of &#x003b2;-amyloid 1-42, total tau, and phosphorylated tau have been observed in adults with PKU, although the long-term clinical implications remain unclear.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27120.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Effective management of PKU depends on early diagnosis, lifelong metabolic control, and comprehensive patient and family education. Universal newborn screening is the primary preventive strategy, allowing prompt initiation of dietary therapy to avert irreversible neurologic injury.</p>
        <p>Patient and caregiver education should begin at diagnosis and continue across the lifespan. Key components include understanding the pathophysiology of PKU, the rationale for lifelong phenylalanine restriction, proper use of medical formula, accurate measurement of dietary protein intake, and recognition of factors that may increase phenylalanine levels (eg, illness and poor dietary adherence). Education should also address label reading, avoidance of aspartame-containing products, and strategies for navigating school, social settings, travel, and transitions to independent self-management. Institutions such as the National PKU Alliance provide strategies and resources to guide affected individuals.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Adolescents and adults require reinforcement of the importance of continued metabolic control, as dietary adherence&#x000a0;often declines with age. Counseling should emphasize the association between elevated phenylalanine levels and cognitive, psychiatric, and neurologic symptoms, even in individuals treated early.</p>
        <p>Women of reproductive age require specific education regarding maternal PKU syndrome and the necessity of strict metabolic control before conception and throughout pregnancy to prevent fetal complications.</p>
        <p>Psychosocial support, access to specialized metabolic dietitians, and connection with patient advocacy organizations can improve adherence to treatment plans and quality of life. Addressing financial barriers to medical foods and pharmacologic therapies is also essential, as access limitations may negatively affect long-term outcomes.</p>
        <p>Overall, sustained education, anticipatory guidance, and interprofessional support are central to preventing complications and optimizing lifelong health in individuals with PKU.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>PKU requires lifelong, coordinated, interprofessional management to optimize neurocognitive outcomes, ensure patient safety, and support treatment adherence across developmental stages. Because PKU is typically identified through newborn screening, frontline clinicians, including pediatricians, family physicians, obstetric providers, and advanced practice clinicians, must understand state screening protocols and ensure timely follow-up of abnormal results. Prompt recognition and referral are critical, as early dietary intervention substantially improves outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Interprofessional Roles and Responsibilities</bold>
</p>
        <p>Effective management is best achieved through a structured team approach. Depending on disease severity and patient age, the team typically includes a primary care clinician, a metabolic geneticist or metabolism specialist, an experienced metabolic dietitian, and a genetic counselor. Additional professionals, including nurses, pharmacists, developmental pediatricians, psychologists, and social workers, are engaged as needed to address medical, developmental, behavioral, and social concerns.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Primary Care Physicians and Advanced Practice Clinicians</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Primary care clinicians coordinate longitudinal care, reinforce adherence, monitor growth and neurodevelopment, and ensure appropriate specialist follow-up. They play a key role in transition planning from pediatric to adult care and in maintaining engagement during adolescence, when dietary adherence often declines.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Metabolic specialists confirm the diagnosis, interpret genotype-phenotype relationships, and develop individualized treatment plans, including dietary prescriptions and selection of pharmacologic therapy. Genetic counselors provide education on inheritance patterns, recurrence risk, family planning, and psychosocial implications.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Registered dietitians (metabolic dietitians) are central to PKU management. They translate phenylalanine targets into practical meal plans, guide formula preparation, supervise dietary adjustments across developmental stages, and help prevent nutritional deficiencies. Ongoing counseling promotes sustainable adherence and supports normal growth.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Nurses support care coordination, reinforce patient education, assist with home management, and facilitate communication between families and the metabolic team. They often serve as accessible points of contact for troubleshooting and follow-up.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pharmacists counsel patients receiving pharmacologic therapy, monitor for adverse effects (including hypersensitivity reactions with pegvaliase), ensure safe medication administration, and assist with insurance navigation and prior authorization processes.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Clinical psychologists, developmental pediatricians, and social workers address executive function challenges, mood disorders, school performance issues, and treatment fatigue. They provide behavioral interventions, psychosocial support, and assistance with financial or insurance-related barriers that may affect adherence.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Women of childbearing age require coordinated care with an obstetrician-gynecologist before conception and throughout pregnancy to ensure strict metabolic control and reduce the risk of maternal PKU embryopathy. Preconception counseling and interdisciplinary collaboration are essential.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Clear, structured communication among team members is essential. Shared decision-making supports patient autonomy, particularly when considering dietary liberalization, pharmacologic therapy, or reproductive planning. Standardized documentation of phenylalanine targets and treatment plans enhances safety and continuity of care.</p>
        <p>Transitions, particularly from pediatric to adult care, are high-risk periods for loss to follow-up. Proactive transition planning, patient education, and coordinated handoffs improve long-term engagement and outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Systems-Level Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Availability of&#x000a0;medical foods, metabolic formulas, and specialty therapies&#x000a0;is&#x000a0;often inconsistent. Healthcare teams should advocate for equitable access and assist families in navigating coverage options and public assistance programs to reduce financial barriers that can compromise metabolic control.</p>
        <p>In summary, optimal PKU outcomes depend on early detection and treatment, sustained interprofessional collaboration, comprehensive patient education, and coordinated care across the lifespan.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27120.s14">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27120&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27120">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://mdsearchlight.com/genetic-disorders/phenylketonuria/?utm_source=pubmedlink&#x00026;utm_campaign=MDS&#x00026;utm_content=27120">Click here for a simplified version.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
